        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Vd9ajincpCws2vnTkcNSbw",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/reflog.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Git Reflog (Reference Logs)\\ndescription: A local safety net that records every position HEAD and branch refs have pointed to, enabling recovery from resets and rebases.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Git\\n  - concept/Recovery\\n  - tech/Git\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single authoritative source describing reflog\\u0027s recovery capability.\\u0022\\nresource: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-reflog\\n---\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nGit\\u0027s reflog (reference log) records every position that HEAD and branch references have pointed to over time. This includes commits that are no longer reachable from any branch after a destructive operation like a reset or rebase.\\n\\n## Recovery Use Case\\n\\nReflog serves as a **local-only safety net** when you\\u0027ve lost commits through:\\n\\n- **Hard resets** \\u2014 \\u0060git reset --hard \\u003Ccommit\\u003E\\u0060 moves HEAD but leaves orphaned commits unreachable from any branch\\n- **Rebases gone wrong** \\u2014 a rebase can leave the original commit chain unreachable if you abort or encounter conflicts\\n- **Branch deletions** \\u2014 deleting a branch doesn\\u0027t immediately erase its commits\\n\\nIn these scenarios, the \\u0022lost\\u0022 commits still exist in Git\\u0027s object database. Reflog lets you recover them by finding their original ref position and checking them out or rebasing onto them again.\\n\\n### How Recovery Works\\n\\n1. Run \\u0060git reflog\\u0060 to see the history of ref movements:\\n   \\u0060\\u0060\\u0060\\n   abc1234 HEAD@{0}: rebase -i: finished rebase\\n   def5678 HEAD@{1}: rebase -i: start\\n   ghi9012 HEAD@{2}: reset --hard origin/main\\n   \\u0060\\u0060\\u0060\\n\\n2. Identify the commit hash (e.g., \\u0060def5678\\u0060) you want to recover.\\n\\n3. Create a new branch or reset to that point:\\n   \\u0060\\u0060\\u0060\\n   git checkout -b recovered def5678\\n   git reset --hard def5678  # if you want to move your current branch\\n   \\u0060\\u0060\\u0060\\n\\n## Local-Only Safety Net\\n\\nReflog is inherently local to your clone:\\n\\n- **Never pushed** \\u2014 reflog entries exist only on your machine; they do not synchronize with remote repositories\\n- **No clone inheritance** \\u2014 a fresh clone of a repository does not include the reflog history from your original clone\\n- **Temporary** \\u2014 entries are eventually garbage-collected, making reflog a time-bounded recovery tool (not a permanent undo mechanism)\\n\\nThis design means reflog is useful for personal recovery but should not be relied on as a backup strategy or for shared team recovery.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: [[sources/steering-adoption-sample-07]]\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
