          "tool_use_id": "toolu_015y3MHYdQ7Kh6DnhvZPdz6M",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022sources/steering-adoption-sample-07.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Source summary\\ntitle: Steering Adoption Sample 07 \\u2014 Git Reflog Recovery\\ndescription: Brief description of git reflog as a local-only safety net for recovering from resets and rebases.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - tech/Git\\n  - concept/Recovery\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Concise statement of reflog\\u0027s purpose as a recovery mechanism; single authoritative source.\\u0022\\nresource: eval://steering-adoption/sample-07\\n---\\n\\n## Source Content\\n\\nGit\\u0027s reflog records every position HEAD and branch refs have pointed to, including commits that are no longer reachable from any branch after a reset or rebase. It\\u0027s a local-only, time-limited safety net \\u2014 reflog entries are never pushed and don\\u0027t exist on a fresh clone.\\n\\n## Key Claims\\n\\n1. Reflog captures all ref movements, including those leading to unreachable commits\\n2. It functions as a safety net for recovery from destructive operations (reset, rebase)\\n3. Reflog is local-only \\u2014 entries do not sync to remote repos or appear in fresh clones\\n4. It is time-limited (entries are eventually garbage-collected)\\n\\n## Coverage\\n\\nThis source focuses on reflog\\u0027s role as a recovery tool. It does not address:\\n- Reflog commands (\\u0060git reflog show\\u0060, \\u0060git reflog expire\\u0060)\\n- Advanced usage patterns\\n- Performance or implementation details\\n\u0022}"
